README.md:7:Use Node.js 22+. Tests use Node built-ins, synthetic secrets, mock HTTP, simulated request streams, and a transactional Firestore fake. They need no credentials, installed SDK, listening sockets, or network access:
README.md:17:All **48 named tests pass** on Node 24.22.1: 20 original webhook/HTTP tests retained, 15 OAuth/encryption tests, 5 bot/chat tests, and 8 Firestore/runtime tests. In this environment `npm test` reports four passing test files; direct invocations expose individual node:test results. This is local verification only. No browser, OBS, login, real SDK, real Firestore, live chat/raid, or deployment validation has occurred.
README.md:19:The dependency `@google-cloud/firestore` is declared in package.json and loaded only when explicitly selecting Firestore. Installation was attempted with empty user/global npm configs and lifecycle scripts disabled. It failed with **`EAI_AGAIN`: `getaddrinfo EAI_AGAIN registry.npmjs.org`** while requesting `@google-cloud/firestore`. The SDK is not installed or integration-tested; a lockfile could not be generated. Resolve registry access before attempting the actual Firestore path. Tests do not substitute a different production backend.
README.md:31:OAuth is an account connection flow for two preconfigured accounts, not a general login/session or admin authorization system. Set `OAUTH_ENABLED=true` only after supplying all OAuth fields. Production fails closed without OAuth configuration and Firestore selection. No send-chat or subscribe control route is publicly exposed.
README.md:40:| `OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI` | Exact registered callback URL ending `/oauth/callback`; no query, fragment, or userinfo. HTTPS required except explicit development `http://localhost`. |
README.md:46:`GET /oauth/streamer/start` requests only `channel:bot`. `GET /oauth/bot/start` requests `user:read:chat user:write:chat user:bot`. These are the minimum scopes for the selected webhook bot design without relying on bot moderator status. `/oauth/callback` exchanges the code using the exact configured redirect, validates client ID, account, scopes and positive expiry, then stores only encrypted access/refresh tokens. Callback errors are generic and never echo provider error descriptions, authorization codes, client secrets, or tokens. The application has no token/request logging; infrastructure request-log redaction still needs review before any live callback URLs are used.
README.md:48:State and browser secrets use 32 random bytes each. Five-minute state records contain only hashes, role and expiry. The state is consumed atomically with a matching browser hash; valid denial/code-error callbacks consume it too. Replay, expiry, cookie duplication, wrong browser, duplicate query parameters, wrong role/account, and unknown callback parameters fail closed. The browser cookie is HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, Path=/; HTTPS uses Secure and the `__Host-` prefix. It is cleared after callback. Starting another flow in the same browser replaces the cookie; complete one connection at a time.
README.md:64:The target remains **Cloud Run scale-to-zero + Firestore**, not Cloudflare. Memory state/auth stores are explicit tests/development choices. `NODE_ENV=production` requires `STATE_STORE=firestore`, `GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=raid-relic-hackathon`, and complete OAuth/webhook settings. Unknown stores/projects or emulator configuration are rejected; SDK or store failures never silently fall back to memory. SDK construction passes the exact project ID. No cloud credentials are provisioned or read by this run. A future authorized cloud runtime would need its operator-managed identity and Firestore access.
README.md:66:The store interface supports atomic event acceptance, ritual reads, create/consume OAuth state, encrypted token reads, and lease-protected token writes. Firestore acceptance transactionally writes event dedupe and ritual/chat mutation together. The fake tests enforce reads before writes, rollback on commit failure, retried transaction callbacks, concurrent duplicate delivery and one-use state across service instances. These tests do not prove real SDK/emulator/database behavior.
README.md:68:OAuth expiry and event replay expiry are checked in application transactions; physical document cleanup/TTL policies are not configured. Firestore currently retains expired records until an operator adds retention cleanup. Latest chat metadata is bounded per configured host. Memory OAuth state has a capacity limit and lazy expiration; memory resets lose state, grants and rituals. Firestore key paths hash external IDs to prevent path injection. OAuth tokens are application-encrypted, while state hashes, role metadata and account/chat IDs are not secret ciphertext.
README.md:74:The owner approved billing linkage and reports the operator is linking billing for `raid-relic-hackathon`. The requested plan is a **£1 GBP monthly budget**, **50/90/100% alerts**, **default billing admin email**, and target **`us-central1`**. These are owner/operator instructions, **not cloud state verified by Codex**. Budget alerts are not a spending cap, and scale-to-zero is not a zero-cost guarantee. No cloud operations, deploys, IAM/billing changes, or costs were initiated by Codex. Do not commit until parent review.
README.md:86:- [Firestore SDK reference](https://docs.cloud.google.com/nodejs/docs/reference/firestore/latest/firestore/firestore): adapter targets `@google-cloud/firestore` transactions. Real SDK installation remains blocked by registry DNS.
README.md:88:Pending before live use: dependency install/lockfile and real SDK tests; app registration and exact redirect validation; actual account grants; callback reachability and webhook delivery; raid subscription permission/condition verification; retention cleanup, request rate limits and log redaction; disconnect/token-key rotation procedures; hourly idle validation if sessions are maintained; subscription reconciliation/revocation lifecycle; user/claim authorization and incoming-raider proof; browser/OBS/accessibility checks; cloud security/cost review and deployment. No browser/live validation is claimed.
README.md:92:Codex authored all source, tests, documentation and the original SVG in this same thread. The initial sandbox blocker was repaired by the operator; work resumed here. The second increment added mocked OAuth, encrypted storage, bot methods and a Firestore adapter tested through injection, with the failed SDK install reported explicitly. Curated `.codex-evidence/run-NNN.jsonl` and `.codex-evidence/SESSION_ID` are permitted; raw backups, other evidence, auth files, secrets and local env remain ignored. No raw backup or credential contents were read, no server was left running, and no commit was created. See [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md).

